// # Lecture 01 – Basics of C++

// ## 1. Introduction to C++

// C++ is a general-purpose programming language used to develop software, applications and games.

// It is also widely used for learning Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA).

// ## 2. Output in C++

// Output means displaying information on the screen.

// In C++, we use `cout` to display output.

// **Syntax:**
// ```cpp
// cout << "Hello World";
// ```

// **Example:**
// ```cpp
// #include <iostream>
// using namespace std;

// int main() {
//     cout << "Hello World";
//     return 0;
// }
// ```

// **Output:**
// ```text
// Hello World
// ```

// ### Important Points

// - `cout` is used to display output.
// - `<<` is called the insertion operator.
// - Text is written inside double quotes.
// - Every statement generally ends with a semicolon (`;`).

// ## 3. How to Move to the Next Line

// We can use `endl` or `\n` to move the output to the next line.

// ### Using endl
// ```cpp
// #include <iostream>
// using namespace std;

// int main() {
//     cout << "Hello Akshit" << endl;
//     cout << "Hello Anchal" << endl;
//     cout << "Hello Aastha" << endl;

//     return 0;
// }
// ```

// ### Using \n
// ```cpp
// #include <iostream>
// using namespace std;

// int main() {
//     cout << "Hello Akshit\n";
//     cout << "Hello Anchal\n";

//     return 0;
// }
// ```

// Both methods move the cursor to the next line.

// ## 4. Printing Text and Numbers

// We can print both text and mathematical expressions using `cout`.

// **Example:**
// ```cpp
// #include <iostream>
// using namespace std;

// int main() {
//     cout << 18 * 2 << endl;
//     cout << "18 * 2" << endl;

//     return 0;
// }
// ```

// **Output:**
// ```text
// 36
// 18 * 2
// ```

// **Explanation:**
// - Without double quotes, C++ evaluates the expression.
// - Inside double quotes, C++ prints the text as it is.

// ## 5. Variables

// A variable is a named memory location used to store data.

// Think of a variable as a container that stores a value.

// **Syntax:**
// ```cpp
// data_type variable_name;
// ```

// **Example:**
// ```cpp
// int x;
// x = 8;

// cout << x;
// ```

// **Output:**
// ```text
// 8
// ```

// ### Variable Declaration and Initialization

// Declaration means creating a variable.

// Initialization means assigning its first value.

// **Example:**
// ```cpp
// int age = 20;
// ```

// Here:
// - `int` = Data type
// - `age` = Variable name
// - `20` = Stored value

// ### Modifying Values of Variables

// We can change the value of a variable during program execution.

// **Example:**
// ```cpp
// #include <iostream>
// using namespace std;

// int main() {
//     int x = 5;

//     cout << x << endl;

//     x = 8;

//     cout << x << endl;

//     return 0;
// }
// ```

// **Output:**
// ```text
// 5
// 8
// ```

// The variable `x` first stores 5 and later stores 8.

// ## 6. Data Types

// Data types define what kind of value a variable can store.

// | Data Type | Description | Example |
// |---|---|---|
// | int | Stores whole numbers | `int age = 20;` |
// | float | Stores decimal numbers | `float price = 10.5;` |
// | double | Stores decimal numbers with more precision | `double pi = 3.14159;` |
// | char | Stores a single character | `char grade = 'A';` |
// | bool | Stores true or false | `bool isPassed = true;` |

// **Important:**
// - `int` stores whole numbers.
// - `float` and `double` store decimal values.
// - `char` uses single quotes.
// - `bool` stores `true` or `false`.

// ## 7. Basic Operators in C++

// Operators are symbols used to perform operations on values and variables.

// ### Arithmetic Operators

// | Operator | Operation | Example |
// |---|---|---|
// | + | Addition | `a + b` |
// | - | Subtraction | `a - b` |
// | * | Multiplication | `a * b` |
// | / | Division | `a / b` |
// | % | Modulus (remainder) | `a % b` |

// **Example:**
// ```cpp
// int a = 10, b = 3;

// cout << a + b << endl;
// cout << a - b << endl;
// cout << a * b << endl;
// cout << a / b << endl;
// cout << a % b << endl;
// ```

// **Output:**
// ```text
// 13
// 7
// 30
// 3
// 1
// ```

// ### Assignment Operator

// The assignment operator `=` is used to assign a value to a variable.

// **Example:**
// ```cpp
// int x = 10;
// x = 20;
// ```

// Now the value of `x` is 20.

// ## 8. Input in C++

// Input means taking data from the user.

// In C++, we use `cin` to take input.

// **Syntax:**
// ```cpp
// cin >> variable_name;
// ```

// Here, `>>` is called the extraction operator.

// ### Example: Taking Integer Input

// ```cpp
// #include <iostream>
// using namespace std;

// int main() {
//     int age;

//     cout << "Enter your age: ";
//     cin >> age;

//     cout << "Your age is: " << age;

//     return 0;
// }
// ```

// **Sample Input:**
// ```text
// 20
// ```

// **Output:**
// ```text
// Enter your age: 20
// Your age is: 20
// ```

// ### Taking Two Inputs

// ```cpp
// #include <iostream>
// using namespace std;

// int main() {
//     int a, b;

//     cout << "Enter two numbers: ";
//     cin >> a >> b;

//     cout << "Sum = " << a + b;

//     return 0;
// }
// ```

// **Input:**
// ```text
// 10 20
// ```

// **Output:**
// ```text
// Sum = 30
// ```

// ## 9. Comments in C++

// Comments are used to explain code. The compiler ignores comments.

// There are two types of comments.

// ### Single-Line Comment
// ```cpp
// // This is a single-line comment
// ```

// ### Multi-Line Comment
// ```cpp
// /*
// This is a
// multi-line comment
// */
// ```

// **Shortcut in VS Code:** `Ctrl + /` to toggle a line comment.

// ## 10. Basic Structure of a C++ Program

// ```cpp
// #include <iostream>
// using namespace std;

// int main() {
//     cout << "Hello World";

//     return 0;
// }
// ```

// ### Explanation

// 1. `#include <iostream>` – Includes the input/output library.
// 2. `using namespace std;` – Allows us to use standard names like `cout` and `cin` without writing `std::`.
// 3. `int main()` – The main function where program execution starts.
// 4. `{ }` – Define the body of the function.
// 5. `cout` – Displays output.
// 6. `return 0;` – Indicates successful program termination.

// ## 11. Important Points to Remember

// 1. `cout` is used for output.
// 2. `cin` is used for input.
// 3. `<<` is the insertion operator.
// 4. `>>` is the extraction operator.
// 5. `endl` and `\n` move output to the next line.
// 6. A variable stores data.
// 7. A data type defines the type of data.
// 8. `=` assigns a value to a variable.
// 9. Comments are ignored by the compiler.
// 10. Program execution starts from `main()`.

